Odin: don't stop a test for an exception nobody meant as fatal (Windows)

`core/testing/signal_handler_windows.odin` registers a vectored exception handler:

    win32.AddVectoredExceptionHandler(0, stop_test_callback)

A vectored handler sees *every* first-chance exception in the process, and
`stop_test_callback` acts on all of them without looking at the code. That is
wrong for any exception that is about to be handled by whoever raised it, and
it is wrong twice over: the test did not fail, and execution continues past the
exception - so the runner then waits for a test that never stops and the whole
binary hangs. In CI that is the job timeout rather than a failed test.

Two sources of benign exceptions, both hit while writing Odin bindings for a
C++ library (Sciter):

  0x4001000A  DBG_PRINTEXCEPTION_WIDE_C - how Windows implements
              OutputDebugStringW. Any library that logs a diagnostic raises it.
              Measured: one CSS warning from the engine killed the test that
              provoked it and hung the binary.
  0xE06D7363  MSVC's C++ exception marker. A C++ library that throws and
              catches internally - ordinary control flow - raises one per throw.

Reproduction with no third-party library at all:

    foreign import k32 "system:Kernel32.lib"
    @(default_calling_convention="system")
    foreign k32 { OutputDebugStringW :: proc(s: [^]u16) --- }

    MSG := [?]u16{'z','z',0}

    @(test)
    on_the_test_thread :: proc(t: ^testing.T) {
        OutputDebugStringW(&MSG[0])          // runner hangs after this
    }

    @(test)
    on_a_spawned_thread :: proc(t: ^testing.T) {
        th := thread.create_and_start(proc() { OutputDebugStringW(&MSG[0]) })
        thread.join(th); thread.destroy(th)  // fine - local_test_index_set is
    }                                        // thread_local and false there

The fix is one early return. The allowlist below is the set of codes that
genuinely mean "this thread cannot continue"; it is deliberately a superset of
the codes `_should_stop_test` classifies, so a fatal-but-unclassified code still
stops the test and is still reported as `.Unknown`.

Verified on Windows 11, Odin dev-2026-08-nightly:

  - both tests above pass, and the runner exits 0
  - a deliberate null dereference is still caught and still reported as
    `Segmentation_Fault`
  - in the binding this came from: one example went from hanging to 11/11
    green, another from a hang to 38/38 green with a `when ODIN_OS != .Windows`
    guard removed

--- a/core/testing/signal_handler_windows.odin
+++ b/core/testing/signal_handler_windows.odin
@@ -65,8 +65,51 @@ stop_runner_callback :: proc "system" (ctrl_type: win32.DWORD) -> win32.BOOL  {
 	return false
 }

+// Is this an exception the test runner should stop a test for?
+//
+// A vectored exception handler sees *every* first-chance exception in the process, including ones that
+// are entirely normal and are about to be handled by whoever raised them. Stopping a test for those is
+// wrong twice over: the test did not fail, and execution continues past the exception - so the runner
+// then waits for a test that never stops, and the whole binary hangs.
+//
+// Two common sources, both measured against a Sciter binding:
+//
+//	0x4001000A  DBG_PRINTEXCEPTION_WIDE_C - how Windows implements OutputDebugStringW. Any library
+//	            that logs a diagnostic raises this.
+//	0xE06D7363  MSVC's C++ exception marker. A C++ library that throws and catches internally - which
+//	            is ordinary control flow - raises one of these per throw.
+//
+// So this allowlist is the set of codes that genuinely mean "this thread cannot continue". It is a
+// superset of the codes `_should_stop_test` classifies below, because a code that is fatal but
+// unclassified should still stop the test and be reported as `.Unknown`.
+@(private = "file")
+is_fatal_exception :: proc "contextless" (code: win32.DWORD) -> bool {
+	switch code {
+	case win32.EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_ARRAY_BOUNDS_EXCEEDED,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_DATATYPE_MISALIGNMENT,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_IN_PAGE_ERROR,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_INVALID_DISPOSITION,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_NONCONTINUABLE_EXCEPTION,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_PRIV_INSTRUCTION,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_STACK_OVERFLOW,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_BREAKPOINT,
+	     win32.EXCEPTION_SINGLE_STEP:
+		return true
+	case win32.EXCEPTION_FLT_DENORMAL_OPERAND ..= win32.EXCEPTION_INT_OVERFLOW:
+		return true
+	}
+	return false
+}
+
 @(private)
 stop_test_callback :: proc "system" (info: ^win32.EXCEPTION_POINTERS) -> win32.LONG {
+	if !is_fatal_exception(info.ExceptionRecord.ExceptionCode) {
+		// Somebody else's exception, and they are about to handle it. Not ours to act on.
+		return win32.EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH
+	}
+
 	if !local_test_index_set {
 		// We're a thread created by a test thread.
 		//
